*.log
.DS_Store
.env
.env.backup
.env.production
# Any hand-made copy of .env holds the same keys the real one does. The three
# patterns above are Laravel's defaults and none of them catches .env.bak,
# .env.old, or .env.2026-09-06 — the names somebody actually types before
# editing a live file. A leaked key is not undone by deleting the file after.
.env.*
# The two exceptions are templates that carry no values — every secret in them
# is the literal text [FILL IN]. The catch-all above stays deliberately broad
# because it is there for .env.bak, .env.old, .env.2026-09-06 — the names
# somebody types before editing a live file — so new exceptions get added one
# at a time, by name, and never as a pattern.
!.env.example
!.env.production.example
.phpactor.json
.phpunit.result.cache
/.fleet
/.idea
/.nova
/.phpunit.cache
/.vscode
/.zed
/auth.json

# Paths carry their folder since the backend/frontend split — the backend's
# composer dependencies and the frontend's npm ones now live one level down,
# and a bare `/vendor` or `/node_modules` here would match neither.
/backend/database/database.sqlite
/backend/public/hot
/backend/public/storage
/backend/storage/*.key
/backend/storage/pail
/backend/vendor
/frontend/node_modules
/frontend/dist
Homestead.json
Homestead.yaml
Thumbs.db

# Debris from `curl` testing against the local site. Both hold real
# credentials — a `primepower-session` cookie and an `XSRF-TOKEN` — and three
# of these were sitting untracked in the working tree waiting to be committed
# to a public repository. Ignored by name rather than trusted to be cleaned up.
cookies.txt
/backend/storage/cookies.txt
/backend/storage/*response*.html
